  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/...\nIssued 212 PM MST TUE NOV 24 2026\nAs an upper-level shortwave grazes our northern areas Friday and \nearly Saturday, precipitation chances 30-60% will span lower \nelevations and 70-90% higher elevations (highest chances to the \nnorth). Snow levels will range 6000-8500 ft MSL with this round of \nprecipitation, highest along the ID/NV and OR/NV borders. Wind \nspeeds and gusts will increase overnight Friday into Saturday as\nthis disturbance moves out of the area. Over the higher terrain of\nSW Idaho, gusts of 30-40 mph are currently forecast. High pressure\nwill build into the region late Saturday into Sunday, bringing dry\nand mild conditions. Valley highs will be near 50 degrees through\nthe weekend, generally 5 to 10 degrees above normal. \n\nBy Monday, an active pattern will return with warm southwest flow \naloft. Models disagree on how far south Canadian cold air will \ntrack, with the current forecast favoring a return to near normal \ntemperatures and snow levels lowering to 5500-6500 ft across the \nregion through next Tuesday. Models remain aligned for the next \nweek on a strong closed low moving into California-Nevada on \nTuesday. The impact over the Intermountain West will depend on\nthe track of the low. Several deterministic solutions favor the\nlow positioned slightly further north, which would increase the \nprecipitation forecast substantially by late Tuesday. Other solutions\npush this low and the moisture south, which would bring mostly dry \nconditions through the middle of the week.",
